WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.626
Do you guys have any thoughts

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:02.961
about your next year?

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:03.920
I feel like it's hard for me

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.046
to think about the
next two months.

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.549
Let alone the next year.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.396
I don't know
if you guys know it.

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:23.857
It's been almost a year
since I joined

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:24.566
Claude Code.

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:26.317
It’s crazy to think about
how far we’ve come

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:28.153
from a year ago
when we were like

00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:30.113
prompting Claude Code,
giving it feedback,

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:32.532
accepting permission
prompts, to now.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:32.991
So, like,

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:35.535
tell me about how you work
with Claude Code right now?

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:35.910
Yeah.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.495
Now I'm doing so
much of my work

00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:39.914
Through Claude Tag,
actually.

00:00:39.914 --> 00:00:42.042
And the kinds of problems
that I'm giving to

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:45.670
Claude Tag are just worlds
more complicated

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:49.674
and challenging than,
you know, implement this class

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:51.051
or write this function,

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.470
which is kind of where
things started at

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.345
with Claude Code.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.597
I think that's
really complementary

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:57.098
to the sort of the shape
of like Claude

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:00.852
Tag living in Slack
as like a Slack native agent,

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:03.104
because it means they can go
and look up

00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:05.065
all this additional context
around like,

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:06.816
hey, what's the product
context?

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:08.568
Like, what are the decisions
that we as a team

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.487
have made about
what this product should be?

00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:14.365
And that just makes its
decision making a lot better.

00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:17.160
Having access to all that
information and understanding

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.497
how to incorporate that
into the choices

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:21.706
that the agent is making.

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:22.123
Yeah.

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:23.333
My work feels pretty similar too.

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.377
I think, 70 to 80% of my work
happens on Claude Tag now,

00:01:26.503 --> 00:01:28.630
and for 20% of the work,
I will maybe

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.547
open up the TUI

00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:31.549
or the desktop app to like,
refine something.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:33.802
Or if I want to be
micromanaging

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:35.637
my Claudes,
I will do that.

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:37.847
I think we've gone from just,

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:39.474
you know,
caring about the transcripts

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:41.810
so much and like,
caring about each individual

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.477
tool call

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:45.271
and each individual decision
that the model makes

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.816
to just a zoomed out
view of, like,

00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:50.693
I have a goal and
I'd like to achieve this goal.

00:01:50.693 --> 00:01:53.113
And you just kind of give
that goal to the model

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:54.739
to achieve for you.

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:55.990
You know,
what I've been reflecting on

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:56.741
is just how much.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:01:59.160
the software development,
life cycle has changed

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.537
and how we've sort of like,

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:03.331
learned all these lessons
along the way,

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:05.250
you know, like building
Claude Code, building

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.253
all these primitives
that tie together.

00:02:08.336 --> 00:02:10.922
Eventually into Claude Tag,
which is like,

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.466
how we're doing everything
from like verification to code

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:17.137
review, like brainstorming, 
monitoring.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.972
Tell me about how working on
Claude Code

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:22.308
compares to working on,
previous software products?

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.436
I mean, there's always
a shelf life on any product,

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:27.814
or the technology underneath
the products rather.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.232
I think the shelf life for
the technology

00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.609
used to be
in the order of years.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:32.402
So it used to be like,

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:33.611
I'm going to make this product

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:36.614
and I have high confidence
that this is going to

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.159
be a thing for,
you know, a few years or more.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:42.162
With models and AI models,
it's like the technology

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:43.955
fundamentally
shifts underneath you

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:45.415
every two months
and it's like,

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:47.083
maybe it's
getting compressed

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:48.835
as time moves forward too.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:52.505
And so it's kind of hard
because you have to stay

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.465
at the frontier
and you have to kind of

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:57.010
go past the frontier really
to like, feel that edge.

00:02:58.261 --> 00:03:00.471
But at the same time,
you have to provide value

00:03:00.471 --> 00:03:02.932
to people
using the models today.

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:04.267
And in the now.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.269
And so it's
this like balancing act,

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:09.272
which is part art, part
science stuff, like how do I,

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.900
how do I build
for this technology

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.194
that's moving
so fast underneath me?

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:16.779
Is there like, a moment
where you felt like

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:19.282
you just caught
the wave in the right part?

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.075
I think the first time
I felt,

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:24.204
the “ride the wave”
feeling was maybe to do lists.

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:25.038
Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:27.999
Like, that was, this was like,
maybe Sonnet 3.5.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.918
And the models just

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:32.629
were not able to do
any kind of long horizon work.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:34.547
And you would give it
like five things to do,

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:37.133
and it would do three things
and just give up.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:38.509
And so that's
when we were like, oh,

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:41.179
maybe you give it a to do list
and it'll do really well.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:42.180
And it did really well.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.182
It was just like, 
whoa, this is

00:03:44.182 --> 00:03:46.935
exactly the thing we need
for this moment in time.

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:49.687
But you know, a year from then,
like, it's disappeared,

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:51.648
like you don't really need the
to do list anymore.

00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:52.398
Like, there's, like,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.192
you know, more complex
memory states

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:56.236
and things
that you can rely on.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:56.778
And I think this is

00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:58.363
the perfect
example of how,

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:00.448
you got to be very
unattached

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.866
to the things
you're building.

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:04.869
Because they're going
to go away fairly quickly.

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:08.039
I think that it's like,
the fact that

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:11.042
we build these things
into the Claude Code harness,

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.627
and a lot of that

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:16.506
is just to cover
for some of the failure modes

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:19.509
that the current state
of the model has.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.512
And as the models get better,

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:24.430
we have the freedom now
to say like, oh, actually

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.015
all these like features
that we built, like

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:27.976
it's not relevant anymore.
We don't need those.

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.269
We can get rid of them.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.396
And the thing that

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:32.939
balances that out
is, okay,

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.233
well now we're doing
even bigger, more challenging

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:36.651
tasks in Claude Code.

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:38.111
And what are the tools
that the model

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:40.405
needs to be able
to stay coherent

00:04:40.405 --> 00:04:43.116
and do a good job on those
even bigger tasks.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.660
And the tools
that we need to give it

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.661
start to look different

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:48.621
as the scope of the
task changes.

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:49.706
A lot of people ask me,

00:04:49.706 --> 00:04:51.582
how does the
Claude Code team work?

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:53.167
And I think one thing
that's unique

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:55.044
about the Claude
Code team is like,

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:57.130
we have to keep up with
the models really closely.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.339
So we need to, like,

00:04:58.339 --> 00:05:00.425
you know,
sometimes make big changes

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:02.719
very quickly where we're like,
oh, the model is so good,

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:03.636
now we need to make

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.389
a whole new proactive agent,
and it needs to be like

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:08.891
a small team
that does it while it's also,

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:09.892
while we're also like,

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:11.561
rewriting the permission
system

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:12.854
and creating artifacts
and things.

00:05:12.854 --> 00:05:14.564
So I think in some ways
we're this

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:17.191
like hyperbolic time
chamber of like,

00:05:17.191 --> 00:05:18.568
where software
development is going.

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:19.652
I don't think most products

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:22.071
hav as short a shelf life.

00:05:22.071 --> 00:05:24.782
You know, and have to keep up
on the edge much.

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:26.367
But definitely
as an engineer,

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:28.161
you kind of have to do
some of this, right?

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:29.329
You have to sort of like,

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.539
you know, understand where
the capabilities are going.

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:33.958
And obviously
we try and help with that

00:05:33.958 --> 00:05:35.460
as much as possible
through the product.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.170
But, yeah, it's
also good to like keep

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.213
like your mental model
of Claude as well.

00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:42.884
I mean, I think when you added
the ask user question tool,

00:05:42.884 --> 00:05:44.719
that was another kind
of example of

00:05:44.719 --> 00:05:46.804
like riding through the wave,

00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:47.597
what were you thinking

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:49.057
when you created
that tool and

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:50.558
what was going on
through your head?

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.810
I wanted Claude to be interactive.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.813
And initially I had it
after planning basically.

00:05:56.105 --> 00:05:57.982
And then I was like,
oh, wait, what

00:05:57.982 --> 00:05:59.734
if it's just a tool
that it can call?

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:01.486
And it took me so long
to design the tool.

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:06.032
Like it was really hard to get
Claude to call the tool well,

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.493
and then eventually, I designed it.

00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:09.243
And then it just started

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:10.870
getting really good
at calling the tool.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.039
And then recently
I'm just like,

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.332
I don’t even use the tool as much.

00:06:14.332 --> 00:06:15.833
I just create an artifact.

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:16.959
And the artifact

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:18.836
asks me questions,
you know,

00:06:18.836 --> 00:06:19.962
and it's like in HTML,

00:06:19.962 --> 00:06:22.006
it has diagrams and mockups
and things like that.

00:06:22.006 --> 00:06:24.384
I'm like, it happened
so quickly, right?

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:25.218
Where it was like,

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:27.428
the tool could barely do it
and now it can.

00:06:27.428 --> 00:06:29.347
But yeah, I think this
happens a lot in Claude Code.

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:30.556
I think that

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.558
the way I think we go
about is like,

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.060
we create these primitives

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.353
where it's like, okay,

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:37.230
how do permissions work?

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.273
How do visualizations work?

00:06:39.273 --> 00:06:40.066
All of these things.

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.818
And then they sort of
layer together.

00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:44.070
Tell me about loops, like,
how do you use them?

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:44.278
I mean,

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:45.655
I think it's interesting

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:46.781
to track the journey of

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:48.991
how we arrived at loops
in some ways. Right?

00:06:48.991 --> 00:06:51.327
It's like, we like,
I used to run everything

00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:52.620
locally on my laptop,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.373
and at some point I had
to go back home,

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:57.542
you know, from the office.

00:06:57.542 --> 00:06:59.460
And so I would have
to shut down my laptop

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.670
and I'd be like,
that sucks.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.130
Like, my agent’s not
running anymore.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.173
You didn’t leave it open?

00:07:04.173 --> 00:07:06.175
Yeah, you could do that
but then I’d have to go

00:07:06.175 --> 00:07:08.636
to the parking lot and like,
people would look at me funny.

00:07:08.636 --> 00:07:10.430
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.473
But what
I started doing then,

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:13.641
was I started using like,

00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:16.352
our remote hosted
developer boxes.

00:07:16.352 --> 00:07:16.727
Yeah.

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:19.522
And so, like,
those were my way of,

00:07:19.522 --> 00:07:21.649
okay, I don't need my laptop
on at all times.

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:22.024
It's just,

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:23.067
it's running and it's running

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:25.361
in a hosted environment,
so I can just do my thing.

00:07:25.361 --> 00:07:25.611
Yeah.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:28.364
But that's also unsatisfying
in some ways,

00:07:28.364 --> 00:07:29.866
because then, like,
you got to, like,

00:07:29.866 --> 00:07:31.742
you know,
ssh into your environments

00:07:31.742 --> 00:07:35.538
and kind of get back
to your hosted surface.

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:37.999
And so that's when,
I guess,

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:39.667
Claude Code on the web
was created. Right?

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.336
So it's like hosted containers
that you have,

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:44.839
that can just keep running
in the background.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:47.758
But even then, like it was,

00:07:47.758 --> 00:07:49.135
it was a little tricky
to get started

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:52.513
because if you want to use
a container to host your,

00:07:52.513 --> 00:07:53.723
host your dev environment,

00:07:53.723 --> 00:07:56.142
you need to give it access
to your dev environment.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:58.269
And setting that up
is a bit of a pain.

00:07:58.269 --> 00:08:00.354
Yeah. It's totally worth it,
in my opinion.

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:01.397
I think it's like, you know,

00:08:01.397 --> 00:08:03.566
10x my productivity
ever since we did that.

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:05.860
But that was kind
of the challenge there.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:06.944
And so I think

00:08:06.944 --> 00:08:08.863
that's the origin story
of like how

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:10.406
we kind of came across this,

00:08:10.406 --> 00:08:12.992
Claude running locally,
to Claude needs to run on the cloud.

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.494
And now if it’s
running on the cloud,

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:15.953
you can do
really interesting things,

00:08:15.953 --> 00:08:18.706
like you can have routines
and you can be like,

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:22.335
you know, every day go and 
look at all the feedback

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:23.377
that we're getting

00:08:23.377 --> 00:08:26.214
and look at, you
know, bucket them into

00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:27.215
buckets of importance

00:08:27.215 --> 00:08:30.218
and fix the ones that
it actually has

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:31.260
high confidence in fixing.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.012
This is the whole like,
loop journey

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:34.639
that Boris talks
about a lot too.

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:38.476
It's like, you go from,
you know, prompting a model

00:08:38.476 --> 00:08:41.103
in a session,
and you do, 

00:08:41.103 --> 00:08:43.231
like breaking that
boundary of like a session

00:08:43.231 --> 00:08:44.398
and instead

00:08:44.398 --> 00:08:47.276
prompting something
that's one level above that.

00:08:47.276 --> 00:08:48.110
Yeah.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.488
That's then going and fixing

00:08:50.488 --> 00:08:52.490
bugs or, like,
doing your bidding for you.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:52.698
Yeah.

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:55.034
I actually wanted to talk
more about code review

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:56.410
you know, and security,

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:57.620
because I think
this is one of those things

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.747
where, okay, you're
writing a lot more code.

00:08:59.747 --> 00:09:02.583
More code
is being generated, and

00:09:02.583 --> 00:09:05.711
you don't want your job to be,
like, reviewing code, right?

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:07.547
So, yeah,
obviously we have like,

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:09.423
our code review bot right now.

00:09:09.423 --> 00:09:10.841
But generally I
wanted to ask you guys

00:09:10.841 --> 00:09:12.635
about like how, you know,
code review has changed

00:09:12.635 --> 00:09:16.180
inside of the team and like,
how we do it right now?

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:20.142
I think one big thing actually
has been, Claude

00:09:20.142 --> 00:09:22.645
really helps human reviewers

00:09:22.645 --> 00:09:25.815
choose where to focus
their time and energy.

00:09:25.815 --> 00:09:29.360
I think like a common thing
that I saw happen

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.322
in traditional human
code review is

00:09:33.322 --> 00:09:35.283
you would have someone go
and review the code

00:09:35.283 --> 00:09:37.076
and they would like,
pick out, like,

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:39.328
you know, three
little nit picks to post.

00:09:39.328 --> 00:09:40.413
And it was kind of just like,

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:44.000
I'm signaling to you
that I read this code.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.501
It’s like, here's
my like three little things

00:09:45.501 --> 00:09:47.295
that I want to point out
to prove that I actually

00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:49.380
was like reading
through this carefully.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:53.050
Now you can have Claude
figure out those little things

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.885
and address them autonomously.

00:09:54.885 --> 00:09:55.970
So I was like,
that's not really

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.222
the important part
of human code review anymore.

00:09:58.222 --> 00:09:58.556
Yeah.

00:09:58.556 --> 00:10:00.224
What is important

00:10:00.224 --> 00:10:02.351
is understanding
maybe the bigger picture.

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:02.893
It's like,

00:10:02.893 --> 00:10:05.646
maybe Claude doesn't
have a full understanding of

00:10:05.646 --> 00:10:08.149
why is this API structured
the way it is?

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:08.649
Or, you know,

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:11.777
the service boundaries
are drawn in a particular spot

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.904
for a reason
that maybe Claude,

00:10:13.904 --> 00:10:17.033
hasn't fully internalized
when I was writing the PR.

00:10:17.033 --> 00:10:18.868
So being able to pull

00:10:18.868 --> 00:10:22.580
information
like that into code review,

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.583
and then hand that
information off to a human,

00:10:25.583 --> 00:10:29.920
I think is a really valuable thing
that Claude can do

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.923
that keeps you from getting
pulled into that little, like,

00:10:32.923 --> 00:10:34.425
oh, I'm just going to, like,

00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:35.926
nit pick something
because I'm, like,

00:10:35.926 --> 00:10:38.220
reading through the code
line by line and,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.014
just level up the

00:10:40.014 --> 00:10:41.474
abstractions
that you're thinking

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:42.808
about as a human code
reviewer.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.435
I think code
review was the first time

00:10:44.435 --> 00:10:46.437
where we did
this big fan out

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:47.438
where we're like,
okay, like,

00:10:47.438 --> 00:10:50.733
how do you find,
you know, every bug, right?

00:10:50.733 --> 00:10:53.444
Or how do you like search
across potential bugs?

00:10:53.444 --> 00:10:55.363
And we do this big fan out
and then,

00:10:55.363 --> 00:10:57.281
you know, coalesce it,
which is like,

00:10:57.281 --> 00:10:58.866
you know, what we call
test time compute, right?

00:10:58.866 --> 00:11:02.703
Like how we use
a lot of inference time

00:11:02.703 --> 00:11:06.457
to put a lot of thought
and compute into a problem.

00:11:06.457 --> 00:11:08.459
And this led to workflows,
right?

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:10.127
So could you tell me
about that Sid,

00:11:10.127 --> 00:11:11.587
and how you built workflows?

00:11:11.587 --> 00:11:16.133
Code review was a big part of
how workflows came about

00:11:16.133 --> 00:11:16.884
in the first place.

00:11:16.884 --> 00:11:17.259
Yeah.

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:18.177
Like we

00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:19.470
we have this,
as you were saying,

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.973
like massive, fan out thing
where it was

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:23.724
kind of like a,

00:11:23.724 --> 00:11:25.101
almost like, a custom harness

00:11:25.101 --> 00:11:28.396
where you tell Claude to go out
and fan out and find bugs.

00:11:28.396 --> 00:11:30.147
And then for each bug,
you might

00:11:30.147 --> 00:11:32.942
do an adversarial review
where you ask it to

00:11:32.942 --> 00:11:35.069
look at the bug
from three different,

00:11:35.069 --> 00:11:35.403
you know,

00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:38.406
opinions or
perspectives and

00:11:38.406 --> 00:11:39.573
see if the bug is
actually real.

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:41.367
What that does is like it
filters out

00:11:41.367 --> 00:11:42.993
all these bugs for you
and gives

00:11:42.993 --> 00:11:44.912
you just
the most important ones

00:11:44.912 --> 00:11:48.207
that require
or need your attention.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:49.458
And it's not just code review.

00:11:49.458 --> 00:11:51.836
Like you could do
the same thing for many,

00:11:51.836 --> 00:11:53.921
many different types
or classes of problems.

00:11:53.921 --> 00:11:54.213
Yeah.

00:11:54.213 --> 00:11:55.715
You could do the same thing

00:11:55.715 --> 00:11:58.259
for performance bugs
or performance issues.

00:11:58.259 --> 00:11:59.552
You could do the same thing

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:01.679
for just general purpose
deep research. Right?

00:12:01.679 --> 00:12:03.431
Like I want to figure out,
like, you know,

00:12:03.431 --> 00:12:04.849
I'm taking my parents to Tahoe

00:12:04.849 --> 00:12:08.561
next week and, like, okay,
where do I, where do I stay?

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:10.604
And you could do, like,
a fan out there and

00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:11.564
that's helpful too.

00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:14.567
Claude is actually really good
at making its own harnesses.

00:12:14.608 --> 00:12:14.859
Yeah.

00:12:14.859 --> 00:12:15.359
Like Claude

00:12:15.359 --> 00:12:16.360
can figure out

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:18.320
what this fan out
should look like,

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.114
what the topology
should look like,

00:12:20.114 --> 00:12:23.617
how it should kind of wire
in the output of one agent

00:12:23.617 --> 00:12:25.661
into the next level,
and then, like,

00:12:25.661 --> 00:12:28.038
finally summarize it
and give it back to me.

00:12:28.038 --> 00:12:30.249
And so that was kind of
the origin story of that as.

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:31.625
And an example is,

00:12:31.625 --> 00:12:32.752
okay, you're planning a trip.

00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:34.295
So you have it like

00:12:34.295 --> 00:12:37.673
do like ten different search
requests across like Tahoe,

00:12:37.673 --> 00:12:41.969
like bathhouse Tahoe, Hotel
Tahoe, Airbnb or something.

00:12:41.969 --> 00:12:43.596
Right?
Places to go.

00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:43.888
Yeah.

00:12:43.888 --> 00:12:45.306
It gives you
a bunch of results.

00:12:45.306 --> 00:12:46.974
And then you have
some agents

00:12:46.974 --> 00:12:48.934
that sort them or

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:51.479
figure out the most
interesting results

00:12:51.479 --> 00:12:52.855
and then maybe
some agents who

00:12:52.855 --> 00:12:54.356
verify them.
Is that right?

00:12:54.356 --> 00:12:55.816
And then it gives you
an end result.

00:12:55.816 --> 00:12:56.525
Exactly.

00:12:56.525 --> 00:12:58.652
Like the problem
is really it's,

00:12:58.652 --> 00:13:01.697
it's like you're fanning out
and once you fan out,

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:03.240
you just have so much
information,

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.576
that you need to
then filter back.

00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:06.869
It's like a MapReduce problem.

00:13:06.869 --> 00:13:07.161
Yeah.

00:13:07.161 --> 00:13:07.745
And you need

00:13:07.745 --> 00:13:09.371
to filter back
for human consumption,

00:13:09.371 --> 00:13:12.666
because if I read the fan out
output I'm going to go crazy.

00:13:12.666 --> 00:13:13.375
Right?

00:13:13.375 --> 00:13:16.921
So you need something to help
build confidence

00:13:16.921 --> 00:13:19.840
and the way to build
confidence in this case

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.635
is just to throw test time
compute at the problem.

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:25.012
And so you kind of build
more confidence that way.

00:13:25.012 --> 00:13:25.805
I think the other thing

00:13:25.805 --> 00:13:28.933
that's really interesting
about workflows is because

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:33.562
the agent is writing code to 
orchestrate the sub agents,

00:13:33.562 --> 00:13:35.397
it's this cool mix of

00:13:35.397 --> 00:13:38.484
deterministic code
defined behavior,

00:13:38.484 --> 00:13:42.530
as well as the, you know,
agentic LLM behavior.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:45.324
And I think that that helps
give me confidence

00:13:45.324 --> 00:13:46.325
when like Claude writes

00:13:46.325 --> 00:13:48.077
a workflow to iterate,
you know, it's like,

00:13:48.077 --> 00:13:50.704
here's a for loop that's going
to run over these items.

00:13:50.704 --> 00:13:52.498
I'm like,
oh yeah, the for loop, it's

00:13:52.498 --> 00:13:54.375
not going to skip
one of the items like it's

00:13:54.375 --> 00:13:56.418
going to apply
the same technique

00:13:56.418 --> 00:13:58.212
to all of these things
equally.

00:13:58.212 --> 00:14:01.048
I find that that's really,
that really improves my,

00:14:01.048 --> 00:14:01.674
my trust.

00:14:01.674 --> 00:14:03.300
Like, oh,
I know that Claude is doing

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.510
what I want it to do here.

00:14:04.635 --> 00:14:06.262
Want to talk about
Claude Tag.

00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:07.471
Like, I know Robert, you're

00:14:07.471 --> 00:14:09.056
building Claude Tag right now.

00:14:09.056 --> 00:14:09.723
But. Yeah.

00:14:09.723 --> 00:14:11.308
How are you using it? Like,
Tell us about it.

00:14:11.308 --> 00:14:12.726
Yeah, yeah, I think,

00:14:12.726 --> 00:14:15.104
so I mentioned earlier,
but we're using Claude

00:14:15.104 --> 00:14:17.773
Tag to build Claude
Tag really aggressively.

00:14:17.773 --> 00:14:18.023
Yeah.

00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:19.817
And that's actually been
a lot of

00:14:19.817 --> 00:14:20.985
my focus is making sure

00:14:20.985 --> 00:14:22.570
that, like, Claude Tag
is unblocked

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.864
on being able to develop
Claude Tag itself.

00:14:24.864 --> 00:14:27.575
Making sure that
the development environment

00:14:27.575 --> 00:14:32.371
and dev loop are really, 
really easy for Claude to use.

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:33.789
It can just, like,
do everything that

00:14:33.789 --> 00:14:37.585
I, as a human need to do
to build the software and test

00:14:37.585 --> 00:14:38.836
that it's working end to end.

00:14:38.836 --> 00:14:42.381
As we build more and more, 
complex, heavily

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:45.050
integrated pieces of software,
that job gets harder, right?

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:48.888
So that making sure that
Claude is able to do that

00:14:48.888 --> 00:14:51.557
sort of thing has been
a big focus for me.

00:14:51.557 --> 00:14:56.437
This is the first time
we've had the user interface

00:14:56.687 --> 00:15:00.232
more fully decoupled
from the transcripts.

00:15:00.316 --> 00:15:00.608
Okay.

00:15:00.608 --> 00:15:01.942
So it's like,
we're actually like,

00:15:01.942 --> 00:15:03.527
we are working now at a level,

00:15:03.527 --> 00:15:06.739
one level of abstraction away
from, like the actual tokens

00:15:06.739 --> 00:15:08.657
that are being output
from Claude.

00:15:08.657 --> 00:15:11.535
So when you see a message
from Claude Tag in Slack,

00:15:11.535 --> 00:15:14.121
it's calling a tool
to send that message to you.

00:15:14.121 --> 00:15:14.330
Yeah.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.373
But its internal monologue
essentially

00:15:16.373 --> 00:15:18.500
is not visible to you
in Slack.

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:20.836
Like there is a link to view
the full transcripts

00:15:20.836 --> 00:15:21.545
from Claude,

00:15:21.545 --> 00:15:26.717
but the primary user interface
is through a messaging tool.

00:15:27.134 --> 00:15:30.012
And that abstraction
was, like, a little scary

00:15:30.012 --> 00:15:30.888
to get through at first.

00:15:30.888 --> 00:15:32.222
We're like, oh, like,
I can't see everything

00:15:32.222 --> 00:15:34.224
that Claude is thinking
as it's like thinking it.

00:15:34.224 --> 00:15:34.516
Yeah.

00:15:34.516 --> 00:15:36.352
But it's also been,

00:15:36.352 --> 00:15:40.189
I think, really freeing in
terms of just like, oh,

00:15:40.189 --> 00:15:43.484
Claude is choosing
what to say to me and when.

00:15:43.484 --> 00:15:46.278
And I'm not thinking
about the details of like,

00:15:46.278 --> 00:15:48.530
which tools is it calling,
what parameters is it

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:50.532
sending to the tools
as it's using them?

00:15:50.532 --> 00:15:56.872
And like being forced
even to let Claude cook.

00:15:56.872 --> 00:16:00.668
And seeing that the models
now are good enough.

00:16:00.709 --> 00:16:01.043
Yeah.

00:16:01.043 --> 00:16:04.088
That I'm getting back
good results without needing

00:16:04.088 --> 00:16:07.091
to supervise the transcripts
in detail.

00:16:07.257 --> 00:16:07.466
Right.

00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:10.344
Is like, it has been a forcing
function of like,

00:16:10.344 --> 00:16:10.970
Oh, yeah,

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:13.097
these models are actually
getting really good now.

00:16:13.097 --> 00:16:13.931
Claude Tag
really clicked

00:16:13.931 --> 00:16:14.556
for me
recently

00:16:14.556 --> 00:16:15.432
because I've been
developing

00:16:15.432 --> 00:16:16.475
a new tool.

00:16:16.475 --> 00:16:17.893
I actually need
a lot of buy in from people.

00:16:17.893 --> 00:16:19.478
So the first thing I tried
was like,

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:21.730
hey, like, I'm
thinking of this idea,

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:22.815
who should I talk to

00:16:22.815 --> 00:16:27.486
who might be interested
in it? Right?

00:16:27.486 --> 00:16:29.947
And then so
I get these stakeholders

00:16:29.947 --> 00:16:31.865
and then I do some mock ups
and it's all in Slack.

00:16:31.865 --> 00:16:33.158
So I can, like,
view it on my phone even,

00:16:33.158 --> 00:16:35.619
it makes mocks 
up for me.

00:16:35.619 --> 00:16:38.205
It does the implementation,
you know, and then,

00:16:38.205 --> 00:16:40.040
but the implementation
is like, you know,

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.041
I don't know if this

00:16:41.041 --> 00:16:42.334
tool really works or not.

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:45.129
So I add a lot of like
events to it. Right?

00:16:45.129 --> 00:16:47.506
And then I,
you know, deploy it internally

00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:49.258
and see like kind of how
people are using it.

00:16:49.258 --> 00:16:51.427
Then Claude Tag
is like monitoring it for me.

00:16:51.427 --> 00:16:53.137
And I'm like,
oh, hey, like,

00:16:53.137 --> 00:16:54.013
how are people using it?

00:16:54.013 --> 00:16:55.139
Is anyone giving me feedback?

00:16:55.139 --> 00:16:56.223
And then I can just like

00:16:56.223 --> 00:16:57.933
drop in instantly
and be like, yes.

00:16:57.933 --> 00:16:59.476
Like, you know, like
Claude tags me

00:16:59.476 --> 00:17:01.645
when someone else
gives me feedback about it.

00:17:01.645 --> 00:17:03.022
And so I can be like
more responsive,

00:17:03.022 --> 00:17:04.982
I can have a higher
confidence that it works.

00:17:04.982 --> 00:17:05.357
Right.

00:17:05.357 --> 00:17:07.359
And then sometimes
I was looking at the events

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:08.986
and I wanted to improve
some of the funnel.

00:17:08.986 --> 00:17:11.530
Like I felt like users
weren't going all the way.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.782
And initially I was like,
I have an idea.

00:17:13.782 --> 00:17:15.159
I'm going to like,
ask it to do this idea.

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:16.076
But then I was like, wait,

00:17:16.076 --> 00:17:18.495
let me ask Claude
to improve the funnel.

00:17:18.495 --> 00:17:18.871
You know,

00:17:18.871 --> 00:17:20.080
like come up
with some ideas here.

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:22.166
And maybe I can give my idea
as an example,

00:17:22.166 --> 00:17:23.083
but I want to sort of,

00:17:23.083 --> 00:17:25.294
like, work with it at that
higher level,

00:17:25.294 --> 00:17:26.837
and that, like, really
clicked for me.

00:17:26.837 --> 00:17:28.922
I think you're touching on
some great points here, right?

00:17:28.922 --> 00:17:32.092
Because effectively
there's verification,

00:17:32.092 --> 00:17:34.219
there's code
review,

00:17:34.219 --> 00:17:37.347
and there's getting feedback.

00:17:37.347 --> 00:17:38.307
So it's like connecting

00:17:38.307 --> 00:17:40.642
to your data store
or your event store

00:17:40.642 --> 00:17:43.020
where you get feedback in
some ways, like maybe metrics

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.564
or maybe it's a Slack,
maybe it's a GitHub issues,

00:17:45.564 --> 00:17:46.690
whatever else it is.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:49.068
And all three of these
are primitives in Claude Code.

00:17:49.068 --> 00:17:49.943
Yes.

00:17:49.943 --> 00:17:51.779
Like verification
is so important

00:17:51.779 --> 00:17:52.613
because like I think we,

00:17:52.613 --> 00:17:55.908
for our team especially,
we have, you know,

00:17:55.908 --> 00:17:58.327
really paid a lot of attention
to verification.

00:17:58.327 --> 00:18:01.955
And so when Claude creates
a PR for me for Claude Code.

00:18:01.955 --> 00:18:05.209
It tests it, it sends me screenshots

00:18:05.209 --> 00:18:07.461
from what it would look like.

00:18:07.461 --> 00:18:09.630
And then
you kind of get confidence.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.548
So in your case too,
like when you were,

00:18:11.548 --> 00:18:13.967
you know, asking you
to verify, like you didn't,

00:18:13.967 --> 00:18:15.052
did you have to open up,

00:18:15.052 --> 00:18:17.721
did you open up the TUI?

00:18:17.721 --> 00:18:18.847
Only to like,

00:18:18.847 --> 00:18:20.641
so first
I asked if to record itself

00:18:20.641 --> 00:18:23.685
using the TUI, you know,
and then after I did that,

00:18:23.685 --> 00:18:25.646
I was like,
I’ll like clone it myself.

00:18:25.646 --> 00:18:26.688
And then try it out.

00:18:26.688 --> 00:18:27.606
Just for sanity’s sake

00:18:27.606 --> 00:18:29.525
I wanted to make sure that I
at least check.

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:32.236
Yeah, just to like,
you know, use it myself.

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:33.737
I feel like eventually,
you know,

00:18:33.737 --> 00:18:36.990
I’ll just let Claude,
not even clone it.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:39.243
I think looking back
at the evolution over the past

00:18:39.243 --> 00:18:40.285
over the part year,
to me it’s

00:18:40.285 --> 00:18:40.911
sort of like

00:18:40.911 --> 00:18:43.080
we started with 
Claude Code and then we

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.040
started adding
these primitives

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:45.582
that would work

00:18:45.582 --> 00:18:48.585
like auto mode and memory
and workflows.

00:18:48.669 --> 00:18:51.380
There was a lot to keep track
of as a developer, right?

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.840
To just be like, oh,

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.675
what's all this stuff
happening?

00:18:54.675 --> 00:18:56.718
But you kind of
have to do that.

00:18:56.718 --> 00:18:57.970
I do think that's like

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.096
the hard part about

00:18:59.096 --> 00:19:00.973
being a software engineer
these days is like,

00:19:00.973 --> 00:19:01.640
it's like

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:02.558
freeing your mind

00:19:02.558 --> 00:19:03.267
kind of like,

00:19:03.267 --> 00:19:05.435
there's so much.
I think you always

00:19:05.435 --> 00:19:07.146
had to do is,
you always have to be like,

00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:09.940
okay, what is possible now?
How do we like, automate more?

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:13.068
But I think, like,
how do we, you know,

00:19:13.068 --> 00:19:15.779
step back and think
bigger, right?

00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:18.323
I think is a really
important part,

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:20.117
but I am curious
if there's, like, anything

00:19:20.117 --> 00:19:22.411
you guys miss about

00:19:22.411 --> 00:19:24.538
the old part of software
engineering?

00:19:24.538 --> 00:19:26.498
And how things used to work.

00:19:26.498 --> 00:19:27.875
For me, I used to get a lot of joy

00:19:27.875 --> 00:19:30.002
from performance engineering.

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:31.086
And so that means, like,

00:19:31.086 --> 00:19:33.005
really digging into a system

00:19:33.005 --> 00:19:34.923
and increasing
its performance.

00:19:34.923 --> 00:19:38.385
I think Claude is much better
at that than I am now.

00:19:38.635 --> 00:19:40.470
But I still get to reap
the benefits

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:43.265
of the actual performance
improvements, which is nice.

00:19:43.265 --> 00:19:45.851
The other thing that I think
I've been focusing a lot on

00:19:45.851 --> 00:19:48.604
is just creating
new ideas quicker.

00:19:48.604 --> 00:19:50.355
So if I have an idea,
going from idea

00:19:50.355 --> 00:19:52.566
to prototype to production
is much faster.

00:19:52.566 --> 00:19:52.733
Yeah.

00:19:52.733 --> 00:19:54.902
And so I think more of
my attention is now going

00:19:54.902 --> 00:19:56.278
on, like new ideas

00:19:56.278 --> 00:19:58.030
and how quickly
I can bring them to life.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:58.947
And so it's like

00:19:58.947 --> 00:20:01.950
I've had to shift
my kind of like paradigm from,

00:20:02.117 --> 00:20:05.078
you know, getting really deep
into the weeds to like,

00:20:05.078 --> 00:20:06.496
more zoomed out
over time.

00:20:06.496 --> 00:20:06.955
Yeah. Yeah.

00:20:06.955 --> 00:20:09.166
Like less mastery
in that specific thing,

00:20:09.166 --> 00:20:11.126
of performance that you’re
spending a ton of time on.

00:20:11.126 --> 00:20:12.544
Exactly.

00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:14.963
I think kind of
a similar thing for me.

00:20:14.963 --> 00:20:19.176
I'm a very, like detail
oriented.

00:20:19.593 --> 00:20:23.013
I love to get into the
weeds of UI design

00:20:23.013 --> 00:20:26.558
for my personal website,
like, I made this,

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:30.354
like super detailed
CSS recreation of the Mac

00:20:30.354 --> 00:20:33.148
OS 10.4 aqua like buttons.

00:20:33.148 --> 00:20:35.734
And I probably spent
like a day

00:20:35.734 --> 00:20:39.196
like getting the exact layer
of, like, radial gradients,

00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:41.949
like stacked up to make
this perfect recreation of it.

00:20:41.949 --> 00:20:42.449
And like,

00:20:42.449 --> 00:20:43.617
that's the type of thing
where I'm like,

00:20:43.617 --> 00:20:46.370
oh, Claude
can just do that for me now.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:49.081
And like, I wouldn't ever go
and do that manually now.

00:20:49.081 --> 00:20:50.165
It kind of brought me

00:20:50.165 --> 00:20:53.627
back to thinking about
when I was probably 7 or 8.

00:20:54.044 --> 00:20:55.921
I really wanted to make
a video game.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:20:56.255
Yeah.

00:20:56.255 --> 00:20:58.298
And, like,
I didn't know how to code yet,

00:20:58.298 --> 00:20:59.883
but I was just like,
you know what?

00:20:59.883 --> 00:21:02.261
I have, like, PowerPoint

00:21:02.261 --> 00:21:04.054
and I can use, like,
PowerPoint to like,

00:21:04.054 --> 00:21:04.596
make, like,

00:21:04.596 --> 00:21:07.599
clickable things and like,
draw these shapes and stuff.

00:21:07.599 --> 00:21:07.766
Yeah.

00:21:07.766 --> 00:21:08.308
It was like

00:21:08.308 --> 00:21:11.561
that was a tool that
I was able to use at that age.

00:21:11.687 --> 00:21:15.315
Thinking now
about how Claude kind of makes

00:21:15.315 --> 00:21:19.152
the entirety of software
engineering accessible to me,

00:21:19.152 --> 00:21:21.905
it's like whatever
idea I have

00:21:21.905 --> 00:21:23.782
I can,
I don't have to be like, oh,

00:21:23.782 --> 00:21:25.200
I don't have the technical
skills.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:28.203
Like I'm not able
to solve this problem.

00:21:28.203 --> 00:21:30.914
Like, I can say, here's
what I want to accomplish.

00:21:30.914 --> 00:21:32.833
Let's like break it
down and figure out

00:21:32.833 --> 00:21:35.836
how to work with Claude
to make it happen.

00:21:35.836 --> 00:21:36.086
Yeah.

00:21:36.086 --> 00:21:38.672
Just everything
kind of inside of that,

00:21:38.672 --> 00:21:41.842
realm of software
engineering feels like

00:21:41.842 --> 00:21:44.511
it's within my grasp now,
which is really exciting.

00:21:44.511 --> 00:21:45.554
I feel like software
engineering

00:21:45.554 --> 00:21:47.556
is like the profession
of change.

00:21:47.556 --> 00:21:48.140
You know, like,

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.892
I think you look back
like 10 or 20 years

00:21:50.892 --> 00:21:52.185
and you're like,
oh, like, you know.

00:21:52.185 --> 00:21:54.771
You're like handwriting
JavaScript without like,

00:21:54.771 --> 00:21:56.023
any sort of frameworks.

00:21:56.023 --> 00:21:57.691
And then you invent
frameworks and,

00:21:57.691 --> 00:21:59.609
you know, compilers
and all of these things.

00:21:59.609 --> 00:22:03.405
And, it's happening faster
now, but it's still like

00:22:03.405 --> 00:22:05.449
fundamentally
software is about

00:22:05.449 --> 00:22:07.701
how do you use these things
to create something

00:22:07.701 --> 00:22:09.995
incredible and
make more

00:22:09.995 --> 00:22:11.580
and more great software,
right?

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:12.664
It's like problem solving.

00:22:12.664 --> 00:22:13.206
Like you say.

00:22:13.206 --> 00:22:13.665
Exactly.

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:14.916
You're solving
different problems,

00:22:14.916 --> 00:22:16.043
but you're still problem
solving.

00:22:16.043 --> 00:22:17.002
Totally.

